# Have you ever cook for others?
Yes, I have. To be honest, I really enjoy cooking for other people, although most of the time I only cook for myself and my family. I'm not an expert cook by any means, so I usually stick to simple dishes such as fried rice, sour soup, or stir-fried vegetables.

I think cooking for others is a great way to show care and appreciation. Whenever I prepare a meal and see people enjoying it, I feel a real sense of satisfaction. It motivates me to keep improving my cooking skills and try out new recipes whenever I have the chance.

# What do we need to prepare when we need to cook?
I think the first thing we need is the right ingredients. Depending on the dish, this could include vegetables, meat, seafood, seasonings, and other essential items. It's always a good idea to prepare everything in advance so the cooking process goes more smoothly.

Besides the ingredients, we also need some basic kitchen equipment, such as knives, chopping boards, pots, pans, and cooking utensils. Having the right tools can make cooking much easier and more efficient.

Another important thing is planning. If you're trying a new recipe, it's helpful to read through the instructions beforehand and make sure you have all the necessary ingredients and equipment ready.

Finally, maintaining good hygiene is essential. This includes washing your hands, cleaning the ingredients properly, and keeping the kitchen tidy to ensure food safety.

# Do you agree that food is an important part of Vietnamese festivals?
Yes, definitely. I think food is one of the most important elements of Vietnamese festivals and celebrations.

For example, during Lunar New Year, families often prepare traditional dishes such as Bánh chưng, pickled vegetables, and various festive foods. These dishes are not only enjoyed for their taste but also carry cultural and symbolic meanings. They represent wishes for prosperity, good health, and family unity.

In addition, preparing and sharing food is a way for family members to spend time together. Many traditional dishes require a lot of preparation, so cooking often becomes a family activity that helps strengthen relationships and preserve cultural traditions.

Overall, I believe food is much more than just something people eat during festivals. It's an important part of Vietnamese culture and plays a key role in bringing people together and creating memorable experiences.

# Which dishes are a must at festivals?
In Vietnam, the dishes that are considered essential often depend on the festival, but some traditional foods are almost always present during major celebrations.

For example, during Lunar New Year, square sticky rice(Bánh chưng) in the North and cylindrical glutinous rice cake(Bánh tét) in the South are must-have dishes. They symbolize family unity, gratitude to ancestors, and wishes for prosperity in the coming year. Families also prepare braised pork with eggs, pickled vegetables, and candied fruits, which are closely associated with the holiday.

For other festivals or family gatherings, dishes may vary by region, but traditional foods are usually preferred because they reflect local culture and customs.

Overall, I think these dishes are much more than just food. They carry cultural and symbolic meanings and help preserve traditions that have been passed down through generations.

# Do you think cooking should be a compulsory or an elective course? Why?
Personally, I think cooking should be taught in schools, but as an elective course rather than a compulsory one.

The main reason is that cooking is an important life skill. Learning how to prepare simple meals can help students become more independent and develop healthier eating habits. It can also teach practical skills such as time management, budgeting, and food safety.

However, not every student is interested in cooking or plans to use it in their future career. Some may prefer subjects related to technology, science, or the arts. Making cooking an elective course would give students the freedom to choose based on their interests and goals.

Overall, I believe cooking is a valuable skill that everyone should have some basic knowledge of, but students should be allowed to decide whether they want to study it in greater depth.

# Are there any differences between cooking today and in the past?
Yes, I think cooking has changed quite a lot compared to the past.

One major difference is the use of technology. Nowadays, people have access to modern kitchen appliances such as microwaves, air fryers, rice cookers, and induction stoves, which make cooking much faster and more convenient. In contrast, people in the past had to rely more on manual methods and spent considerably more time preparing meals.

Another difference is the availability of ingredients. Thanks to globalization and modern transportation, people can easily buy ingredients from different parts of the world and experiment with a wider variety of cuisines. In the past, people mainly used local and seasonal ingredients.

I also think lifestyles have changed. Many people today have busy schedules, so they often choose ready-made meals, takeout food, or food delivery services instead of cooking from scratch. As a result, home cooking may not be as common as it was for previous generations.

Overall, while modern cooking is much more convenient and diverse, traditional cooking often involved more time, effort, and family participation.
